---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
	title="About Steven Yule"
	description="Steven Yule, chartered civil engineer in Dubai, with 17 years making digital and AI useful on live capital programmes across the GCC and the UK."
>
	<div class="page-head">
		<h1 class="page-title">About Steven Yule</h1>
	</div>

	<div class="prose">
		<picture class="about-photo">
			<source
				type="image/webp"
				srcset="/steven-yule-portrait-360.webp 360w, /steven-yule-portrait-720.webp 720w"
				sizes="(min-width: 40em) 13rem, 9rem"
			/>
			<img
				class="portrait"
				src="/steven-yule-portrait-360.jpg"
				srcset="/steven-yule-portrait-360.jpg 360w, /steven-yule-portrait-720.jpg 720w"
				sizes="(min-width: 40em) 13rem, 9rem"
				alt="Steven Yule"
				width="360"
				height="450"
			/>
		</picture>
		<p>
			I am Steven Yule, a chartered civil engineer based in Dubai. I lead
			digital, data and AI work for infrastructure at director level. For 17
			years I have worked in the Gulf and the UK on making technology useful
			once it meets a live capital programme.
		</p>
		<p>
			I have built and led teams in digital strategy, data engineering, data
			science and software delivery. I also ran a regional digital and data
			practice. That meant answering for its growth as well as the quality of
			its work. Running a practice taught me to judge a digital idea on two
			counts. Will it work on a real asset? Can the organisation buy it and
			afford to run it for years? The second question is usually the harder
			one.
		</p>
		<p>
			Most digital capability stalls in the same place. It is proven in a
			pilot and treated as finished because it worked once. The real test is
			whether a live programme can depend on it. I spend most of my time on
			that gap. It is usually settled
			earlier than people expect, in the procurement and in who owns what at
			handover. Large owners meet the same gap at portfolio level. Each
			development can be relied on alone while the portfolio as a whole misses
			its range.
		</p>
		<p class="pen-mark">
			The clearest example in my own work is Riyadh. As project director with
			the Riyadh Infrastructure Projects Center, I led the first integrated
			infrastructure plan for the region. It brought the works programmes of
			more than 20 government and utility bodies into one plan covering
			thousands of projects. Road space and working time could then be booked
			once by agreement. Until then they had been contested site by site. The
			plan is publicly credited with preventing more than 5,000 conflicts
			between their works.
		</p>
		<p>
			The work is commissioned in three ways. An <a href="/review/"
				>independent review</a
			> gives an owner a written opinion on a decision before it is confirmed.
			Continuing advisory covers decisions that keep arriving over months. A
			non-executive or board appointment is for challenge that has to happen
			in the boardroom. A review is the simplest place to start. All three are
			described under <a href="/work/">Work</a>.
		</p>
		<p>
			My background covers transport, utilities, smart cities and the built
			environment. It also covers data-centre enablement and asset-heavy
			capital programmes in the UK and the Gulf.
		</p>
		<p>
			I am a Fellow of the Institution of Civil Engineers (CEng FICE), an ICE
			Policy Fellow and the ICE UAE Country Representative. I am also a Fellow
			of the Chartered Institution of Highways and Transportation (FCIHT) and
			a member of the Institute of Asset Management (MIAM). I am a Freeman of
			the Worshipful Company of Engineers and a non-executive director of
			Thomas Telford Ltd.
		</p>
	</div>

	<div class="origin-note">
		<h2 class="origin-note-title">About the name</h2>
		<p>
			OkArthur is named after my son Arthur, who is seven and asks more
			questions, and sharper ones, than I am always ready for. Ok, Arthur
			became the answer, and then it became the name. Taking a question
			seriously, and not settling for the defensible answer, is most of what
			good advice turns out to be.
		</p>
	</div>
</Base>
